<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN>

<PLUGIN
  name="fanctrlplus2"
  author="ck9393 / andrebrait"
  version="1.9.7"
  launch="Settings/fanctrlplus2"
  pluginURL="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/main/plugin/fanctrlplus2.plg"
  min="6.9.0"
  support="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/issues"
  project="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus"
  readme="https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus#readme"
>

<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/images/fanctrlplus2.png
</ICON>

<CHANGES>
<![CDATA[

### 1.9.7

## What's changed

- **Warning when a configured sensor gives no reading** (#21). A sensor without a reading cannot drive the fan curve, so a fan whose only source was missing sat at its Idle or Min Speed while the dashboard looked normal. The plugin now sends one Unraid warning per fan per boot when a configured CPU or auxiliary sensor stays unread for at least 60 seconds, naming the sensors. This covers a missing file or tool, a failing or timed-out custom script, output that is not a temperature, and impossible values. Each sensor is timed on its own, so sensors failing on alternate rounds do not trigger a false warning. Disks are not included: a spun-down disk has no temperature by design. (#23)
- **CPU sensor readings no longer carry over.** A round whose CPU reading is unusable used to reuse the previous round's temperature; it now counts as 0 °C. (#23)
- **A notification that fails to send is retried** on the next round, for this warning and for the nct6687 stuck-fan warning. (#23)

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes. Fan speeds behave as before, apart from an unusable CPU reading counting as 0 °C instead of the previous round's temperature.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. Tests cover which sensors count as unread, the per-sensor 60-second timer, one notification per boot, and the retry after a failed send. The real loop, run with a missing CPU sensor and a missing `storcli`, sent one notification naming both after 60 seconds. It has not yet been checked on a real Unraid server.

### 1.9.6

## What's changed

- **Sensors read through an external tool recover after boot** (#21). The fan loop looked for `storcli`, `nvidia-smi` and `mget_temp` only once, when it started. If another plugin or boot script installed the tool a few seconds later, that sensor gave no reading until the service was restarted, and a fan with no other source stayed at Idle Speed. The loop now looks for these tools on every reading, so a late tool is used from the next reading on. (#22)

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. A new test shows that a sensor whose tool is missing gives no reading, and that it gives one as soon as the tool is installed. This test fails on 1.9.5. The fix has not yet been checked on a real Unraid server with a real `storcli`.

### 1.9.5

## What's changed

- **No more writes to the flash drive every 10 seconds** (#19). While the array was started, the array monitor rewrote its lock file on the USB boot drive on every pass, about 17,000 writes a day. The lock now lives in RAM at `/var/run/fanctrlplus2.config.lock`, so taking it never writes to the flash drive. (#20)
- **Only one array monitor runs.** The installer started a second copy after the service had already started one, so two ran after every install and boot. (#20)

## Upgrade compatibility

No configuration changes. The installer removes the old `/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/.config.lock`.

## Verification

All 28 test files pass. The old monitor reset the lock file and updated its modification time on every pass. The new one leaves the file untouched and still starts the fan service. The settings page and the monitor still exclude each other through the new lock. It has not yet been checked on a real Unraid server; the flash write counter in `/sys/block/sda/stat` (field 5) should stop rising by about 12 per minute.


For older releases, see https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases
]]>
</CHANGES>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
old_manifest="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus.plg"
old_runtime="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus"
old_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus"
new_config="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2"
marker="$new_config/.migration-v1"

if [[ ! -e "$marker" ]]; then
  mkdir -p "$new_config"

  if [[ -d "$old_config" ]]; then
    for old_cfg in "$old_config"/fanctrlplus_*.cfg; do
      [[ -f "$old_cfg" ]] || continue
      old_name="${old_cfg##*/}"
      new_cfg="$new_config/fanctrlplus2_${old_name#fanctrlplus_}"
      [[ -e "$new_cfg" ]] || cp -p "$old_cfg" "$new_cfg"
    done

    if [[ -f "$old_config/order.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/order.cfg" ]]; then
      sed 's/fanctrlplus_/fanctrlplus2_/g' "$old_config/order.cfg" > "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" &&
        mv "$new_config/order.cfg.tmp" "$new_config/order.cfg"
    fi

    if [[ -f "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" && ! -e "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg" ]]; then
      cp -p "$old_config/pwm_labels.cfg" "$new_config/pwm_labels.cfg"
    fi
  fi

  touch "$marker"
fi

if [[ -f "$old_manifest" || -d "$old_runtime" ]]; then
  echo ""
  echo "FanCtrl Plus configuration imported into FanCtrl Plus 2."
  echo "Uninstall upstream FanCtrl Plus, then install FanCtrl Plus 2 again."
  echo "Installation stopped to prevent both plugins controlling the same fans."
  echo ""
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-1.9.7-noarch-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new --reinstall">
<URL>https://github.com/andrebrait/fanctrlplus/releases/download/1.9.7/fanctrlplus2-1.9.7-noarch-1.txz</URL>
<SHA256>5aa323531859fdacd7596c57a2bcae5c29253efc2673ee4a9acb4015ce8b04bd</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
for package in /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-*.txz; do
  [[ "$package" == "/boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/fanctrlplus2-1.9.7-noarch-1.txz" ]] || rm -f "$package"
done

src="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2"
dst="/etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2"
if [[ -f "$src" ]]; then
  ln -sf "$src" "$dst"
fi

mkdir -p /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2
rm -f /var/tmp/fanctrlplus2/*
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
rm -f /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2/.config.lock # the lock now lives in /var/run

# start() launches array_monitor.sh (pgrep-guarded); do not start it again here.
if [[ -x "$dst" ]]; then
  "$dst" restart
fi

echo ""
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo " FanCtrl Plus 2 is installed."
echo " Version: 1.9.7"
echo "------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
<![CDATA[
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/rc.fanctrlplus2 stop 2>/dev/null
pkill -f "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2/scripts/array_monitor.sh"
removepkg fanctrlplus2 2>/dev/null || true

rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/fanctrlplus2
rm -f /etc/rc.d/rc.fanctrlplus2

echo "============================================================"
echo "    FanCtrl Plus 2 has been removed."
echo "    Reboot to restore fan control to BIOS or other tools."
echo "============================================================"
exit 0
]]>
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
